I hardly ever use it, but reading the source indicates that
it is for stealing gold-- you run some chance of angering
any non-tame creature from which you steal.

Chance of success goes as follows:

start with 5.  If you are a rogue, add twice your level to this.
If your dex is less than 10, subtract 1 (3 if you are a rogue) from
the above for each point of dex less than 10 that you have.
If your dex is above 14, add 1 (3 if you are a rogue) to the
above for each point of dex you have over 14.

If you are wearing gloves which are not gauntlets of dexterity,
subtract 5.  If you are not wearing gloves at all, add 5 (*).

If you are using a shield, subtract 10.

Adjust for armor based on the weight below:
  weight   adjustment
  0-75      +10
  75-125    +5
  125-175   +0
  175-225   -5
  225-275   -10
  275-325   -15
  325-375   -20
  375+      -25
Note: If you are not wearing armor, you get no bonus (*).

If the result is less than 5, it becomes 5. 
If the result is greater than 95, it becomes 95.

The result is your percent chance of success- you automatically
succeed on a tame creature. 

If you succeed, you will steal (yourlevel * (d25 + 24))  gold
(limited by the gold carried by the creature, of course).
If you succeed [even if the target has no money] and the target is not tame,
you exercise your dexterity.


If you fail, the monster becomes angry.


(*) These two things seem rather strange to me-- if you are wearing
    gloves of dex, then you are better off taking them off than
    wearing them to steal, unless they are +5 (breakeven) or better
    [which generally doesn't happen], or you are a rogue.
    Furthermore, if you are not wearing any armor, it should
    count for the same bonus as wearing very light armor.

